--- body ---

## Context

Below-index reads can be successful task recall rather than failed orientation. Replace the single ranking-miss target with component-attributed diagnostics and behavioural comparisons.

Design baseline: v6, merged by PR #903. See `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design.md` and `docs/2026-09-11-memory-implementation-plan.md`. This planning change implements nothing and closes no work.

## Relevant files

Publication/delivery records; common fixtures from 0923; v6 §§8 and 12; tests/evaluation artefacts in normal project locations.

## Actions

1. Define representative tasks and outcome rubrics before running comparisons. Separate mechanical delivery gates from semantic correctness.
2. Compare disabled memory, orientation alone and full delivery with matched corpus/model/settings; repeated runs report denominators and uncertainty.
3. Attribute observed reads to orientation, recent supplement and task recall using UUID/revisions and actual delivered context rather than a daily default index.
4. Evaluate half/baseline/double channel budgets, one component at a time; record controlled and cumulative tokens, latency, stale-advice use, unknown applicability and ledger routing.

## Test

Positive control removes a known useful lesson and changes the relevant measurement; no-read run reports unavailable rate. A successful below-cut recall is not classified as orientation failure by definition.

## Verification

- [ ] Run the focused fixtures and show they detect the relevant failure.
- [ ] Run applicable repository checks; record commands, results and limitations.
- [ ] Confirm the implementation matches this scope and v6, including explicit capability limits.

## Invariants

- Canonical bodies and provenance remain reviewable; memory cannot override decisions.
- No mandatory residency, automatic generalisation, new native-memory modes or policy duplication.
- Use the common library and normal Git integration; no runtime implementation is part of the planning PR.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Reproducible evaluation protocol and first-runtime results reported; later adapter results attached as available.
- [ ] No invented universal success threshold or vendor score equivalence.
- [ ] Calibration recommendation distinguishes proposals from adopted budget policy.
